# A DOUBLE RELEASE, and it is the most serious thing this counter catches
# (M-marked-acquisition, 2026-09-14). The binding below is entirely correct:
# `slot_open` says `acquires slot_close`, `slot_close` says `consumes`, and
# `check/acquiring.hero` is satisfied. The PROGRAM hands the same handle back
# twice, so the count goes to -1 and the exit says so.
#
# **This case exists because the message was wrong for it.** Until this day the
# negative sentence named exactly one cause, *a `consumes` whose producer
# carries no mark*, which was the only one there was when the counter landed.
# Run against this program it accused the declaration, sending its reader to
# edit an `extern` while the program was releasing twice. Three causes were run
# that afternoon and the message named one; it now names all three, worst
# first, and this is the worst.
#
# Why the counter and not a rule: `selfhost/check/leasing.hero:29` and
# `selfhost/check/consuming.hero:22` both state that the checker has no flow
# analysis, and *how many times does this path run* is a flow question.
#
# **Since 2026-09-25 the second release goes through a copy** (panel 177's
# item 3, route M (must)). The checker has one flow analysis now,
# `check/moved.hero`, and `slot_close(a)` twice through ONE NAME is what it
# refuses at check time, `handle_used_after_end`; that shape lives in
# `tests/golden/check/`
# `handle-ended-on-every-path-is-refused-at-the-read.hero`. The copy is the
# shape the rule cannot see, design.md Part 8 wart 20, and it is what keeps
# this case the runtime's: the count still goes to -1 and the exit still says
# so. The sentence above was true when written and is kept as the record.

#
# **The sentence this case asserts moved on 2026-09-25 (panel 177's items 1 to
# 3, defects 077 and 088).** The second release still stops before C runs, and
# the check that stops it is the DEAD SET: the first `slot_close` ended the life
# and poisoned `a`, but the second goes through `twice`, the copy the checker's
# half routed it through, which still holds the old address, and the runtime
# remembers that address as ended. The poison's own sentence is pinned by the
# `dead-handle-*` cases, the one-name shape is refused at check, and the
# runtime's stray report by
# `fixedbugs-a-double-release-before-any-acquisition-is-stopped-before-c`,
# whose handle nothing ever emptied.
extern "abort-handle-given-back-twice.h"
    record Slot tag Slot
    function slot_open(n: i64) -> Slot acquires slot_close
    function slot_close(s: Slot consumes)
    function slot_value(s: Slot) -> i64

function main()
    a = slot_open(n: 7)
    print(slot_value(s: a))
    twice = a
    slot_close(a)
    slot_close(twice)
